WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.140 --> 00:00:04.420
Every image you've seen of a black hole,

00:00:04.420 --> 00:00:05.720
including this one,

00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:07.380
is now science fiction.

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:10.100
Astronomers have successfully snapped

00:00:10.100 --> 00:00:13.240
the first genuine picture of a black hole.

00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:16.820
[music]

00:00:18.340 --> 00:00:20.900
Black holes in their own don't look like very much.

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:23.400
They don't reflect light because

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:26.560
any light that falls into them can never escape.

00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:29.200
And they don't emit any light that we can detect.

00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:30.600
Those aren't the only reasons

00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:32.820
black holes are hard to photograph.

00:00:33.100 --> 00:00:35.380
Even though they're really, really massive--

00:00:35.380 --> 00:00:37.820
they can be millions or billions of times

00:00:37.820 --> 00:00:39.160
the mass of the Sun.

00:00:39.160 --> 00:00:41.200
They don't take up that much space on the sky.

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:42.800
They've compressed all that mass

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:44.800
into a really tiny little space.

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:47.800
The closest being black hole to Earth

00:00:47.800 --> 00:00:49.460
is at the center of the Milky Way,

00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:52.200
which is 26,000 light-years away from us.

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:55.100
And so in order to be able to see something

00:00:55.100 --> 00:00:58.440
of that size we need a telescope that

00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:00.820
has a resolution that is powerful enough

00:01:00.820 --> 00:01:03.960
that it could see a doughnut on the surface of the Moon.

00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:07.540
The black hole illustrations we're all familiar with

00:01:07.540 --> 00:01:09.060
aren't complete lies.

00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:12.040
Plenty of data suggest that black holes exist.

00:01:12.040 --> 00:01:14.820
Some even shoot jets into space that we can see

00:01:14.820 --> 00:01:17.660
and spaghettify stars that get too close.

00:01:17.660 --> 00:01:20.480
So far, the best evidence for these behemoths

00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:22.020
has been her entourage.

00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:26.560
They wrap huge amounts of gas

00:01:26.560 --> 00:01:29.200
and like charged plasma around them.

00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:32.380
And as that stuff is trying to fall into the black hole,

00:01:32.380 --> 00:01:33.800
it can't all go in at once,

00:01:33.800 --> 00:01:35.720
so it orbits super super super fast.

00:01:35.720 --> 00:01:39.800
And the friction in that fast orbiting heats it white-hot.

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:43.800
Most illustrations focus on this accretion disk.

00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:46.020
But now we're looking at the real deal

00:01:46.020 --> 00:01:49.720
thanks to the event horizon telescope, or the EHT.

00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:54.200
This monumental first image features the black hole

00:01:54.200 --> 00:01:56.840
at the center of a galaxy called M87.

00:01:57.380 --> 00:02:00.760
At more than 50 million light years from Earth,

00:02:00.760 --> 00:02:01.880
it's farther away than

00:02:01.880 --> 00:02:04.120
the black hole at the centre of the Milky Way

00:02:04.120 --> 00:02:05.400
but more massive.

00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:07.660
That makes it easier to image.

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:10.540
The picture shows the shadow of the black hole

00:02:10.540 --> 00:02:13.920
on the glowing gas and plasma in the accretion disk.

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:16.100
The black hole shadow creates a silhouette

00:02:16.100 --> 00:02:19.580
on the disk that outlines the point of no-return --

00:02:19.580 --> 00:02:20.740
the event horizon.

00:02:20.780 --> 00:02:22.600
Taking such a picture requires

00:02:22.600 --> 00:02:25.260
a really, really big telescope.

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:29.480
The EHT uses a lot of smaller radio telescopes instead.

00:02:31.740 --> 00:02:34.320
They work together using a technique called

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:36.500
very long baseline interferometry.

00:02:36.500 --> 00:02:38.320
And what that does is it makes those telescopes

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:41.320
kind of act like one giant telescope.

00:02:41.900 --> 00:02:44.180
Scientists analyzing the data can see

00:02:44.180 --> 00:02:46.500
how the light waves detected by each telescope

00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:48.440
line up with each other to figure out

00:02:48.440 --> 00:02:50.740
what's light from the edge of the black hole

00:02:50.740 --> 00:02:52.300
and what's noise.

00:02:53.220 --> 00:02:55.600
The EHT's first image gives us

00:02:55.600 --> 00:02:58.160
an up close look at the accretion disk,

00:02:58.160 --> 00:03:00.180
plus two key bits of information --

00:03:01.080 --> 00:03:04.280
the size and shape of the black hole's shadow.

00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:07.920
And then that will be enough to answer

00:03:07.940 --> 00:03:09.960
a lot of interesting astrophysical questions

00:03:09.960 --> 00:03:11.220
about how black holes work,

00:03:11.220 --> 00:03:12.720
how event horizons work

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:14.040
and what is going on there.

00:03:14.440 --> 00:03:16.300
The big questions include:

00:03:16.300 --> 00:03:18.700
how exactly do accretion discs form

00:03:18.700 --> 00:03:20.500
and feed black holes?

00:03:20.500 --> 00:03:23.100
Where the black hole jets originate?

00:03:23.380 --> 00:03:25.800
And the oldie but always a goodie...

00:03:25.800 --> 00:03:28.020
was Einstein right?

00:03:28.020 --> 00:03:30.420
His general theory of relativity predicted

00:03:30.420 --> 00:03:32.280
that event horizons could conceal

00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:34.780
tiny massive objects like black holes.

00:03:35.460 --> 00:03:37.780
The shape of this image that we see

00:03:37.780 --> 00:03:39.660
is something that you can predict

00:03:39.660 --> 00:03:41.520
using general relativity.

00:03:42.660 --> 00:03:45.900
The EHT's closeup doesn't defy Einstein.

00:03:46.460 --> 00:03:49.280
Our understanding of gravity still holds.

00:03:49.280 --> 00:03:51.400
But there will be more to come.

00:03:52.400 --> 00:03:55.080
The team hopes to take better and better images

00:03:55.080 --> 00:03:57.600
of M87's black hole and others --

00:03:57.600 --> 00:04:00.000
including the one at the center of the Milky Way,

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:02.000
Sagittarius A*.

00:04:02.480 --> 00:04:04.460
Those images might have something new to say

00:04:04.460 --> 00:04:06.460
about general relativity as well as

00:04:06.460 --> 00:04:09.600
the physics theory that deals with very small objects,

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:10.920
quantum mechanics.

00:04:10.920 --> 00:04:13.320
The two of them don't play well together.

00:04:13.320 --> 00:04:15.580
We don't understand how gravity works

00:04:15.580 --> 00:04:17.260
on sort of quantum levels.

00:04:17.260 --> 00:04:19.860
And if we see something that differs

00:04:19.860 --> 00:04:21.120
from our expectation,

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:23.120
That could help point us to

00:04:23.120 --> 00:04:26.720
one of many theories that try to reconcile

00:04:26.720 --> 00:04:29.500
quantum mechanics and general relativity.

00:04:30.540 --> 00:04:32.280
It's also just cool.

00:04:32.920 --> 00:04:34.720
Whether it's a pale blue dot

00:04:34.720 --> 00:04:37.140
or pristine portrait of Pluto,

00:04:37.400 --> 00:04:39.440
seeing has always offered us

00:04:39.440 --> 00:04:41.840
a new perspective on the universe.

00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:44.400
Humans are very visual,

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:46.400
and so we love having images.

00:04:46.400 --> 00:04:48.680
And I think having an actual picture of a black hole

00:04:48.680 --> 00:04:51.420
is just going to blow people's minds.

00:04:54.880 --> 00:04:55.800
Hi! I'm Helen.

00:04:55.800 --> 00:04:57.740
I make videos here at Science News.

00:04:57.740 --> 00:05:00.500
If this video looks a little bit familiar to you,

00:05:00.500 --> 00:05:03.120
it's because we released a version a few weeks ago.

00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:04.320
At the time we thought that

00:05:04.320 --> 00:05:08.180
the EHT's first image would be of Sagittarius A*,

00:05:08.180 --> 00:05:10.500
the black hole at the center of the Milky Way.

00:05:10.500 --> 00:05:14.300
But it turned out to be of M87's black hole instead.

00:05:14.300 --> 00:05:17.320
So we decided to release a new version of the video

00:05:17.320 --> 00:05:18.760
with the new results.

00:05:18.760 --> 00:05:22.300
The EHT still hopes to get an image of Sagittarius A*.

00:05:22.300 --> 00:05:24.040
It just won't be the first image

00:05:24.040 --> 00:05:25.880
of a black hole ever released.

00:05:26.160 --> 00:05:27.340
Am I going to like break continuity

00:05:27.340 --> 00:05:28.760
if I roll up my sleeves?

00:05:28.760 --> 00:05:29.900
Nah, I'm gonna start this one over.

00:05:29.900 --> 00:05:30.700
This is hard.

00:05:30.700 --> 00:05:31.340
I was just watching

00:05:31.340 --> 00:05:33.480
the Great British Baking Show last night.

00:05:33.580 --> 00:05:34.240
Umm.

00:05:34.240 --> 00:05:36.840
They wear the same clothes two days in a row.

00:05:36.840 --> 00:05:38.200
We'll go there, yeah.

00:05:38.200 --> 00:05:39.600
I feel like it's wrong.

00:05:39.600 --> 00:05:41.880
Sorry, yeah you can cut that. [Laughs]

00:05:41.880 --> 00:05:43.680
So don't roll up your sleeves then.

00:05:43.900 --> 00:05:45.880
Something like that...

00:05:45.960 --> 00:05:47.960
[Laughing]

00:05:49.060 --> 00:05:51.120
[music]

